  "B_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u)**3*(1+sqrt(1 + r/((1+u)**2)))**2)",
  "C_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u))",
  "derivation": "Derived a smooth two-slot extension of the source u=0 body. Let z=1+u and s=sqrt(1 + r/((1+u)**2)). The exact local square-root increment from z to sqrt(z^2+r) is g_exact = sqrt(z^2+r) - z = r/(z*(1+s)). Inside the full chi support the apparatus gives g = r/2 + r^2*B + u*r*C. Choose C so that r/2 + u*r*C = r/(2*z), namely C = -1/(2*(1+u)). The remaining r^2 coefficient is then B = -1/(2*(1+u)**3*(1+s)**2). Using r/((1+u)**2) = s^2 - 1, one checks that r/(2*z) - r^2/(2*z**3*(1+s)**2) = r/(z*(1+s)), so g = g_exact and (z+g)^2 = z^2 + r = p exactly when chi_r*chi_u = 1. At u=0, C contributes zero through u*r*C and B reduces to the source body -1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))**2). On the closed evaluation rectangle r in [-1/2,1/2] and u in [-1/4,1/4], z is in [3/4,5/4] and the square-root argument is at least 1/9, so both bodies are C-infinity on an open neighborhood of the rectangle. In the transition zones the correction is a smooth blend between the linear r/2 and the exact increment; where chi_r or chi_u is zero, g = r/2. This is a local coefficient identity only; it is not a global or full NS claim.",
  "inspect": [],
  "note": "Successor should check: (1) Exact cancellation holds only where both cutoffs are active; partial cutoff gives a smooth blend and zero cutoff gives r/2. (2) The sqrt domain requires 1 + r/((1+u)**2) > 0 and 1+u != 0; the current rectangle has margin (minimum argument 1/9), but a future state could hit the domain boundary before chi_r fades, causing NO_APPLIED_UPDATE at that point while other points continue. (3) The generation-2 rows show the C=0 program leaves O(u*r) whole residuals; this revision removes that leading cross term and should reduce in-support residuals to rounding level. (4) Large out-of-support values, including negative or huge z, are not addressed by the coefficient bodies because the cutoffs are zero; they are state/source geometry, not program failures. (5) No analytic certificate is attached; this does not certify the full PDE or NS theorem."
